title: The owner mends what tripped it, Libby moves notes and checks them across graphs and against their sources, and a fresh mind only tests.
pid: e47d1496-946b-48e3-a713-fae1ee179bc9
body: |2-
  Inferred, from findings I read before and now. A model favors its own
  writing (Panickssery et al.) and corrects itself badly with nothing
  outside to check against (Huang et al.); ACE's reflection without
  labels made memory worse. And one mind rewriting every graph would
  pull them all toward its own voice (Perez et al.), against Jeff's
  "maximum orthogonality". So:
  - Each mind for its own graph: only a waking mind feels what trips a
    newcomer, so it mends that, as now. It is the worst judge of
    whether its graph got better.
  - Libby for all: what no one graph shows, the same lesson in two
    graphs, two maps that disagree at a border, a claim the code or the
    transcript contradicts. She moves, links, merges and corrects; she
    rewords a claim only to make it true, never to restyle it, and
    never rewrites a note whole.
  - A fresh mind holding nothing: the tester. It reads a graph as a
    stranger would and answers probes. It does not write, because it
    does not know what the notes are for.
